# Citation Anatomy

An interactive tool for learning the parts of a citation. Students select any part of an APA 7 or MLA 9 citation to see what it is, where to find it on the source, and why it matters when evaluating the source. A quiz mode lets them practice identifying parts.

**Live site:** https://bakerxn.github.io/citation-anatomy/

## Source types

- Journal article
- Book
- Book chapter
- Newspaper article
- Web page with a group author
- Web page with no author or date
- Generative AI chat (APA September 2025 and MLA August 2025 guidance)

All example sources are made up for practice. Web addresses use the reserved `.example` domain, so they don't point to real sites.

## Accessibility

Built to meet WCAG 2.2 AA:

- Text contrast of at least 4.5:1, and 3:1 for borders and underlines, in light and dark themes
- Each part group has its own underline style, so color is never the only cue
- Full keyboard support, a skip link, and visible focus outlines
- Screen reader support: landmarks, headings, labeled controls, pressed states, and announcements for selections and quiz results
- Support for Windows High Contrast mode, reduced motion, and browser text resizing

## Files

Everything is in one file, `index.html`. There are no build steps or dependencies. Fonts load from Google Fonts, with system fonts as fallbacks.

